# Full reference: docs/config.md
#
# Copy to .env and `docker compose --profile minimal up -d`. The hostnames below
# are Compose service names, because that is what a container resolves — this
# file is loaded *into* the containers, so `localhost` here means the container
# itself and nothing else. Running the processes directly on your machine
# instead? Replace postgres/qdrant/redis/parser with localhost, and the ports
# match what Compose publishes.
#
# Nothing here is a real credential. Every value is a development one, and
# docs/config.md explains which of them become secret-store references in
# production.

NACRE_ENV=development
# **The front door, and reachable from the machines that use it** — not just
# from the server.
#
# `8082` rather than the API's `8080`, because the `web` service proxies `/v1`,
# `/.well-known`, `/oauth` and `/mcp` to the right process behind it. One origin
# serves the console, the API, the authorization server and MCP, so this one
# value answers every question the others used to need their own variable for:
# the console's address, the OAuth issuer, the consent redirect and the resource
# identifier are all derived from it or from the request.
#
# The API's own port is still published and still works. This is the address
# that needs to be right, not the only one that exists.
#
# `localhost` is right only while everything using it is on this machine.
# Reaching the stack over a VPN or a LAN means putting that address here — a
# client told `localhost` goes looking on its own machine and finds nothing.
NACRE_CANONICAL_URL=http://localhost:8082
# What the MCP transport calls itself. **Deliberately unset**, and the absence
# is the setting.
#
# Left unset, the transport builds its discovery document from the `Host` each
# client actually used, which is what RFC 9728 asks the identifier to match: the
# client compares it against the URL it connected to and refuses before sending
# a token. Set, it is pinned — and pinned to `http://localhost:8081` is right
# only for a client on the server's own machine. Everyone reaching the stack at
# `10.8.0.1` or a hostname gets a refusal with a wrong value in it.
#
# It was set here, and `docker-compose.yml` says in a comment that omitting it
# from the `mcp` service is the fix. That comment was true of the service block
# and false of the stack: `env_file: .env` is on the shared anchor, so this line
# reached the container anyway and switched the derivation off for every
# deployment that copied this file. The fix is here.
#
# Set it only when a proxy rewrites `Host` and the public name is something the
# server cannot see. It moves the discovery document only; the audience and
# issuer below are what a token is checked against.
# NACRE_MCP_CANONICAL_URL=
# Browser origins the MCP transport answers. Empty refuses every browser and no
# agent, which is the right default: validating Origin is required to stop DNS
# rebinding, and an agent sends none.
NACRE_MCP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=
# The identity provider in front of this installation, if there is one. Named in
# the RFC 9728 discovery document so a client knows where to get a token.
#
# Left unset the document names **this installation's API**, which since 0.5.3
# is an authorization server: it runs the consent screen where a person picks
# the agent an MCP client will act as. Set this only to hand that job to
# somebody else's provider.
# NACRE_OAUTH_AUTHORIZATION_SERVER=

# Host-side ports Compose publishes api, mcp and the admin UI on. Read by
# `docker compose` for interpolation, never by the product — the ports inside
# the network stay 8080/8081/80 whatever these say, so probes and in-network
# references are unaffected. Set one when the default collides with something
# already listening on your machine. NACRE_WEB_HOST_PORT is the origin the admin
# UI is served on, same-origin with the API it proxies.
# NACRE_API_HOST_PORT=8080
# NACRE_MCP_HOST_PORT=8081
# NACRE_WEB_HOST_PORT=8082

NACRE_PG_URL=postgres://nacre:nacre@postgres:5432/nacre
NACRE_QDRANT_URL=http://qdrant:6333
NACRE_REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/0

# The full profile only, so these stay commented — this file's default is the
# minimal profile the quickstart runs, and minimal starts no object store.
# Setting NACRE_S3_ENDPOINT is what turns object storage on; leaving it on under
# minimal points ingest at a MinIO that is not running, and the first upload
# fails with a 500 rather than storing the bytes inline. Uncomment all four
# together for `--profile full`.
#
# The bucket named here is created for you on `--profile full`, by the
# minio-init one-shot beside MinIO in docker-compose.yml. Pointing these at an
# S3 that is not that MinIO means the bucket is yours to create: the first
# ingest into one that does not exist fails with NoSuchBucket, and /v1/ready
# reports `s3: false` before it does.
# NACRE_S3_ENDPOINT=http://minio:9000
# NACRE_S3_BUCKET=nacre
# NACRE_S3_ACCESS_KEY=minioadmin
# NACRE_S3_SECRET_KEY=minioadmin
# NACRE_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true

# Required, and deliberately empty. Set it before the first `up`.
#
# minimal ships no embedder — that is what keeps it runnable on a laptop without
# a GPU — so this names something you provide. It used to default to the `full`
# profile's embedder service, which meant the documented minimal first run came
# up healthy, accepted a document, and failed to index it against a hostname
# that does not exist in that profile. A default naming a host the deployment
# does not have is the exact failure the "no silent defaults for URLs" rule in
# docs/config.md is about: the process looks healthy, takes traffic, and reports
# the problem as an error rate.
#
# Empty, the API and worker refuse to start and say which variable is missing.
#
#   full:     NACRE_DEFAULT_EMBEDDING_ENDPOINT=http://embedder:80
#   minimal:  any OpenAI-compatible /embeddings endpoint you run
#
# A base URL, and a path on it is kept — `https://api.openai.com/v1` and
# `http://host.docker.internal:11434/v1` (Ollama) call /v1/embeddings, while
# `http://embedder:80` calls /embeddings. On an Apple Silicon Mac the second of
# those is the arrangement; see docs/apple-silicon.md.
#
# Make NACRE_DEFAULT_EMBEDDING_DIM match the model, or the index is built with
# the wrong width and every search misses.
NACRE_DEFAULT_EMBEDDING_ENDPOINT=
NACRE_DEFAULT_EMBEDDING_MODEL=bge-m3
NACRE_DEFAULT_EMBEDDING_DIM=1024

# Not a Nacre variable — Compose passes it to the embedder and reranker (Text
# Embeddings Inference). The airgapped profile sets it to 1 so those containers
# use the pre-seeded models volume and make no outbound request; leave it 0 for
# full, which is expected to fetch the weights on first boot.
# HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1
NACRE_PARSER_ENDPOINT=http://parser:8090

# Where a browser is sent to choose which agent an MCP client will act as.
#
# **Leave it unset.** It defaults to `/#/consent` on NACRE_CANONICAL_URL, which
# is the front door above — the same origin that serves the console. Behind an
# ingress that has always been right; behind this stack it is right now that
# `web` proxies `/oauth` as well as the bundle.
#
# It was a third address naming `localhost`, and the one nothing can derive:
# `/oauth/authorize` knows the host the browser used and not the port the
# console is published on. Reached from another machine it sent the person to
# their own. Making the console and the authorization server one origin is what
# removed the question rather than answering it again.
#
# The fragment is a **route**: the admin UI is hash-routed, so `#/consent` names
# the screen and the authorization request is appended to it. A value with no
# fragment works too, for a deployment serving that screen at its own path.
#
# The flow it starts issues a token for a **service account**, never for the
# person who approves it: an agent is a principal with its own grants, and
# handing it your authority would answer "what may this agent read" with
# "whatever you may read".
# NACRE_OAUTH_CONSENT_URL=

# The signing key for every token this installation issues.
#
# Symmetric and in plaintext because this is the development profile; anything
# else is a key that everyone who has ever cloned the repository holds. Change
# it before the installation is reachable by anyone but you.
NACRE_JWT_SECRET=development-only-not-a-secret-0123456789
# Set only while rotating the one above, and unset again afterwards. See
# docs/config.md — leaving it set indefinitely keeps a retired key valid.
# NACRE_JWT_SECRET_PREVIOUS=
#
# The other mode, and the one to prefer once this is more than a laptop:
#
#   openssl genpkey -algorithm ed25519 -out /run/secrets/jwt_ed25519
#   NACRE_JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_REF=file:///run/secrets/jwt_ed25519
#
# The key that verifies is then not the key that signs, so only the process
# issuing tokens needs the private half and the public one is published at
# /.well-known/jwks.json. Setting it alongside NACRE_JWT_SECRET is refused at
# startup: they are two answers to "what signs a token".
# NACRE_JWT_PRIVATE_KEY_REF=
# NACRE_JWT_PREVIOUS_KEY_REF=
# Both go into every token this installation issues. The issuer must match
# NACRE_CANONICAL_URL in production, and neither can be changed afterwards
# without invalidating every token already out there.
NACRE_JWT_ISSUER=http://localhost:8080
NACRE_JWT_AUDIENCE=nacre

# Reranking buys more than any chunking tuning, and needs a service to send the
# request to. The full profile provides one; minimal does not, so this stays off
# rather than pointing at something that is not running.
#
# On, this fetches NACRE_RERANK_CANDIDATES results from the index, scores them
# against the query with a cross-encoder, and returns the best top_k. That
# widening is not the post-filter invariant 2 forbids: every candidate has
# already passed the permission filter inside the index traversal, so the trim
# is on relevance and changes which permitted results come back, never how many.
NACRE_RERANKER_ENABLED=false
# NACRE_RERANKER_ENDPOINT=http://reranker:80
NACRE_RERANK_CANDIDATES=50
# Sign-in. The refresh TTL has to be longer than the access one, or a session
# ends at its first refresh; the application refuses to start otherwise.
NACRE_ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL=900
NACRE_REFRESH_TOKEN_TTL=2592000
# Counted per email address rather than per organization: there is no
# organization until a sign-in succeeds, and what this defends is one account's
# password.
NACRE_RATE_LOGIN_PER_15MIN=10
# And per client, which is what the line above does not do: one password sprayed
# across ten thousand addresses never repeats a key and never meets that limit.
# Looser, because an office behind one NAT is one client here.
NACRE_RATE_LOGIN_SOURCE_PER_15MIN=60
# How many proxies sit in front of the API, for the limit above. 0 ignores
# X-Forwarded-For and uses the socket address, which is right for development
# and for anything reached directly. Behind an ingress set it to the number of
# hops it adds — counted from the right, because each proxy appends and the
# left-hand entries are whatever the client sent.
NACRE_TRUST_PROXY=0
# A bearer token required on /metrics. Unset — the default — leaves the endpoint
# open, which is right when the port is on an internal network and wrong the
# moment the API is behind a public ingress with /metrics not carved out.
# At least 16 characters if set.
NACRE_METRICS_TOKEN=

# How long the effective-principals cache holds an entry. A memory bound and
# not a correctness one: the key carries the organization's permission epoch, so
# a grant change composes a different key rather than waiting for this to
# expire. `NACRE_ACL_PROPAGATION_SLA` sat beside it until migration 0016 and is
# gone with the tag cache it bounded — there is no propagation to wait for.
NACRE_ACL_CACHE_TTL=60

# How long a tombstoned document keeps its vectors before the collector removes
# them. Not a correctness knob: a deleted document leaves search immediately,
# because `deleted = false` is in every query. Short here so a development
# database does not accumulate points nobody will look at.
NACRE_GC_GRACE=60
# A document claimed for longer than this is treated as abandoned and requeued —
# a worker that died between claiming and finishing leaves no other trace.
# Must exceed the slowest legitimate indexing run.
NACRE_INDEX_LEASE=900
# After this many claims the document is failed instead of requeued, so one
# document that kills the worker cannot keep killing its replacements.
NACRE_INDEX_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5
# How long a reindex keeps the collection it replaced. It is the rollback
# window: moving the pointer back is instant and works only while that
# collection still exists. Each one retained is a full copy of the
# organization's vectors, so this is a disk cost as much as a safety margin.
NACRE_COLLECTION_RETENTION_DAYS=7
# The recall a reindex must reach before its layer switches onto the new model,
# as a whole number out of 100. Applies only to a layer that has a reference
# query set — PUT /v1/layers/{id}/reference-queries — because the check needs
# documents someone picked, and there is no gate before that. 0 means measure
# without blocking, which is arithmetic rather than a special case: every recall
# clears a floor of zero and the number is still recorded.
NACRE_REINDEX_MIN_RECALL=80

# Commercial modules to load, comma-separated. Empty here and empty by default:
# this repository is complete with nothing loaded, and the modules live in a
# separate private repository under a different licence.
#
# A name that cannot be imported stops the process. Naming a module means the
# deployment is paying for what it does, and starting without it would be
# silently a different product.
NACRE_MODULES=
